{
  "myth": "In the beginning, the sky was a flat, starless void. A lonely fox named Koda lived on the highest peak, longing for the warmth of the sun that disappeared during the long winters. He climbed to the highest mountain, scraping his silver-furred tail against the rocky summit. Each time his brush hit the stones, sparks flew—not of fire, but of concentrated starlight. As he ran across the horizon, his tail swept the clouds, igniting the frozen air with vibrant ribbons of emerald, violet, and crimson. The gods, moved by the fox’s devotion to brightening the dark, granted him the power to paint the night forever. Now, whenever the world grows coldest and the silence of winter deepens, Koda dances across the celestial dome. He reminds the mortals below that even in the deepest shadow, light is never truly gone. The Aurora is Koda’s promise, a shimmering trail of his journey through the stars, guiding those who have lost their way back to the hearth of the world. It is the fox’s breath, turning the frigid night into a canvas of eternal, ethereal hope."
}

